From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Reindert Pelsma <reindert.pelsma@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 19:43:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] ui/gtk: do not grab the pointer on a click

A plain left click in the guest window took the host's pointer and
keyboard, hid the cursor, and did not reach the guest.  The window was
usable again only after Ctrl+Alt+G.  Nothing the user did asked for
that.

gd_button_event() grabbed on the first left click whenever the current
input device was relative, and returned TRUE, so the click that
triggered the grab was also swallowed rather than forwarded.  Upstream
means this as a convenience for a guest with only a PS/2-style mouse,
where nothing tracks the host cursor and a grab is the only way to point
at anything.  It is the wrong default the moment a guest has a desktop:
there, a click is a click, and the pointer must be free to leave the
window afterwards.

It is also the one grab path a guest could reach without the user
choosing it.  The guest decides which input device is current -- QEMU
keeps one current mouse handler and it is whichever was activated last,
which happens when the GUEST enables the device -- so a guest that
enabled a relative mouse armed this branch, and then the user's next
click handed over the pointer.  Not an escape (the click is still the
user's, and Ctrl+Alt+G still releases), but the guest chose the
precondition, and "every grab is a user gesture" reads better when it is
the whole truth.

After this, entering grab mode is the View menu item or Ctrl+Alt+G, and
nothing else.  That is the model the two modes are specified against:
ungrabbed the pointer belongs to the host and clicks are forwarded as
they are to any window; grabbed the guest has the pointer until the user
asks for it back.  It is the same split VirtualBox has.

Removing the block leaves `s` unused in this function, so it goes too.
Grab On Hover is untouched: it is an explicit opt-in menu setting and it
takes only the keyboard.

VERIFIED: builds --enable-gtk (NVKVM_QEMU_UI=1), no new warnings.  NOT
verified interactively -- the machine this was written on has no display.
The change is a deletion of one branch, so what it does is visible in
the diff; what has not been watched is a real click landing in a real
guest.

NOT upstreamable as-is: upstream wants this behaviour for the
mouse-only case, and dropping it outright would regress those guests.
The upstreamable version is a display option (`-display gtk,
grab-on-click=off`) defaulting to today's behaviour, which is a bigger
change than nvkvm needs.
---
 ui/gtk.c | 20 +++++++-------------
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ui/gtk.c b/ui/gtk.c
index daa3dd6..23ca23f 100644
--- a/ui/gtk.c
+++ b/ui/gtk.c
@@ -988,21 +988,15 @@ static gboolean gd_button_event(GtkWidget *widget, GdkEventButton *button,
                                 void *opaque)
 {
     VirtualConsole *vc = opaque;
-    GtkDisplayState *s = vc->s;
     InputButton btn;
 
-    /* implicitly grab the input at the first click in the relative mode */
-    if (button->button == 1 && button->type == GDK_BUTTON_PRESS &&
-        !qemu_input_is_absolute(vc->gfx.dcl.con) && s->ptr_owner != vc) {
-        if (!vc->window) {
-            gtk_check_menu_item_set_active(GTK_CHECK_MENU_ITEM(s->grab_item),
-                                           TRUE);
-        } else {
-            gd_grab_pointer(vc, "relative-mode-click");
-        }
-        return TRUE;
-    }
-
+    /*
+     * nvkvm: no implicit grab here.  Upstream took BOTH pointer and keyboard on
+     * the first left click whenever the current device was relative, and
+     * swallowed that click.  A click is a click: it is forwarded to the guest
+     * like any other, and the grab is entered only by the View menu item or
+     * Ctrl+Alt+G.  See this patch's commit message.
+     */
     if (button->button == 1) {
         btn = INPUT_BUTTON_LEFT;
     } else if (button->button == 2) {
